In C, you can terminate a program in several ways, depending on what you need:

```c
return 0;
```

Use this at the end of `main()` to indicate successful completion:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Done.\n");
    return 0;
}
```

You can also use `exit()` from `<stdlib.h>`:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    exit(0);   // successful termination
}
```

Common conventions:

- `return 0;` or `exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);` — normal/successful termination
- `return 1;` or `exit(EXIT_FAILURE);` — termination due to an error

Example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    int ok = 0;

    if (!ok) {
        fprintf(stderr, "An error occurred.\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

Prefer `return` when you are already inside `main`. Prefer `exit()` when terminating from a helper function, since `return` would only leave that function rather than end the whole program.
